      "evidence_type": "published tag insertion in the same loop or domain",
      "functional_impact_measured": "Tagged endogenous TfR used for live-cell imaging and trafficking studies in SUM159 cells; surface display confirmed. Transferrin uptake and endocytic function were not explicitly quantified for the tagged allele versus wild-type.",
      "confidence": "medium",
      "rationale": "L4 reports CRISPR-mediated endogenous tagging of TfR with SBP+EGFP for surface-display experiments. No residue is named, but TfR1 topology places the sole extracellular terminus at the C-terminus (residue 760, 'F'). Python confirms seq[759]='F' and that position 760 falls within the extracellular span (87\u2013760). The position is therefore topology-derived, not chosen: only one extracellular terminus exists. CRISPR-Cas9 knock-in at the endogenous TFRC locus in SUM159 breast cancer cells fused SBP and EGFP to TfR; the construct is full-length and surface-displayed. The clip names no residue, but TfR1 is a type II single-pass protein with its N-terminus intracellular (residues 1\u201365) and its C-terminus extracellular (residue 760); the C-terminus is the only extracellular terminus available for a fusion.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "pmid": 33523982,
          "citation": "PMID 33523982",
          "claim": "The CRISPR-Cas9 strategy that we previously described ( 22 ) was used to engineer the breast cancer\u2013derived SUM159 cells to express endogenous TfR fused to the streptavidin-binding peptide (SBP) and enhanced green fluorescent protein (EGFP)."
        }
